1
00:00:13,332 --> 00:00:17,675
[SPEAKER_01]: This is from the Anguttara Nikaya Book of Sixes, verse 13.

2
00:00:17,735 --> 00:00:21,598
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called the Elements of Escape.

3
00:00:22,639 --> 00:00:24,621
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read the first one.

4
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhikkhus, there are these six elements of escape.

5
00:00:30,984 --> 00:00:31,505
[SPEAKER_01]: What six?

6
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:33,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just the first one.

7
00:00:34,348 --> 00:00:44,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Take a bhikkhu who says, I've frequently practiced and developed a liberated mind through non-ill-will or metta.

8
00:00:46,109 --> 00:00:55,281
[SPEAKER_01]: I've cultivated it, made it my vehicle and my basis, kept it up, consolidated it and properly implemented it.

9
00:00:55,822 --> 00:00:59,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, somehow, ill will still occupies my mind.

10
00:01:01,689 --> 00:01:02,931
[SPEAKER_01]: That bhikkhu should be told.

11
00:01:03,895 --> 00:01:04,516
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

12
00:01:04,656 --> 00:01:05,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Not so, Venerable.

13
00:01:05,637 --> 00:01:06,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't say that.

14
00:01:06,798 --> 00:01:10,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't misrepresent the Buddha, for misrepresentation of the Buddha is not good.

15
00:01:11,103 --> 00:01:12,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha would not say that.

16
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:13,726
[SPEAKER_01]: It is impossible.

17
00:01:14,907 --> 00:01:23,376
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot happen that the mind, liberated through non-ill-will or metta, which has been developed, would still be occupied by ill-will.

18
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:29,250
[SPEAKER_01]: For it is the mind that is liberated to non-ill-will that is the escape from ill-will.

19
00:01:30,531 --> 00:01:42,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's exactly the same principle of when we said many times before that I don't know what the dhamma is, as in I'm not a sotāpanna, but I'm practicing the dhamma.

20
00:01:44,303 --> 00:01:45,023
[SPEAKER_00]: How's that possible?

21
00:01:46,585 --> 00:01:46,745
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

22
00:01:47,145 --> 00:01:48,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Metta means non-ill-will.

23
00:01:50,048 --> 00:01:52,330
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're practicing metta, ill-will cannot be arising.

24
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:57,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So it means I cannot do metta if I'm not free from it?

25
00:01:57,337 --> 00:01:57,497
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

26
00:01:57,717 --> 00:01:59,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It means don't think you're doing metta.

27
00:02:01,119 --> 00:02:06,782
[SPEAKER_00]: You should keep trying to do metta, but don't think that's the metta for as long as you're having your will.

28
00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:12,365
[SPEAKER_00]: You should be trying to practice the dhamma, but don't think that's the dhamma for as long as you're not a sotāpanna.

29
00:02:13,265 --> 00:02:14,306
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

30
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:17,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly... because people will think, oh, so I shouldn't do this.

31
00:02:17,968 --> 00:02:20,670
[SPEAKER_00]: No, do it, just don't think that it is what it isn't.

32
00:02:21,753 --> 00:02:24,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because if I think I'm doing the metta, that's good enough.

33
00:02:24,914 --> 00:02:30,137
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not going to make effort to find out what the real metta is, that would prevent ill-will from arising.

34
00:02:30,857 --> 00:02:37,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you read what one moment of metta practice is like, vast amounts of merit.

35
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:38,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I practice metta.

36
00:02:38,761 --> 00:02:38,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

37
00:02:39,121 --> 00:02:39,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there you go.

38
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:40,922
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm already set.

39
00:02:41,102 --> 00:02:47,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But I practice metta, but is my mind... like, do I know the non-arisen ill-will?

40
00:02:49,122 --> 00:02:50,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I maintain the mind?

41
00:02:50,662 --> 00:02:52,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I prevent ill-will from arising?

42
00:02:53,183 --> 00:02:58,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see, like, oh, my mind is free from ill-will now, but can ill-will arise?

43
00:02:59,205 --> 00:03:00,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't know.

44
00:03:00,685 --> 00:03:01,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you can.

45
00:03:01,586 --> 00:03:02,886
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know, it means you can.

46
00:03:03,786 --> 00:03:15,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, like the way we say, not just that you're restrained, withdrawn from centrality, you have to keep the context over your mind, whereby, oh, can non-arisen centrality arise in different circumstances?

47
00:03:15,029 --> 00:03:17,370
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, am I still liable to centrality?

48
00:03:18,510 --> 00:03:22,497
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, means you're not practicing fully, rightly, correctly.

49
00:03:22,918 --> 00:03:24,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't understood the escape from it.

50
00:03:25,102 --> 00:03:25,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So how can...

51
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:29,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I am practicing the escape of sensuality, but I don't really know what the escape of sensuality is.

52
00:03:30,536 --> 00:03:38,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I am practicing free from ill-will, but mine, throughout that time, remains liable to future ill-will, means you're still within the domain of ill-will.

53
00:03:38,463 --> 00:03:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a good thing to know.

54
00:03:40,725 --> 00:03:41,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

55
00:03:41,286 --> 00:03:47,692
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is not in what you're doing in terms of attempting your practice, or the problem is not solely based on that.

56
00:03:47,792 --> 00:03:52,696
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is simply you thinking that it is the matter.

57
00:03:53,577 --> 00:03:56,259
[SPEAKER_00]: That it is full freedom from sensuality, even momentarily.

58
00:03:56,659 --> 00:04:00,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Because to that extent, you won't search for what real metta is.

59
00:04:01,042 --> 00:04:03,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you would block your own development.

60
00:04:04,085 --> 00:04:05,346
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be striving further.

61
00:04:05,526 --> 00:04:06,947
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be striving further.

62
00:04:07,587 --> 00:04:11,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And people would be usually too keen to jump on.

63
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:14,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, this is the dhamma, although I'm not a sotāpanna.

64
00:04:14,272 --> 00:04:17,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes, this is metta, although I am liable to future real-world arising.

65
00:04:17,955 --> 00:04:20,456
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason I'm practicing metta is because I'm angry.

66
00:04:20,536 --> 00:04:22,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they would jump onto that because

67
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:33,796
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very kind of uncomfortable to keep maintaining the notion that there is more, there is more, this is not enough, this is not enough, this is not enough.

68
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:37,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you just... Oh, but this is enough.

69
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:41,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, look, my life's much better now.

70
00:04:41,063 --> 00:04:42,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but are you liable to future real world?

71
00:04:42,805 --> 00:04:42,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

72
00:04:43,065 --> 00:04:46,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Means it's not the method that the Buddha was teaching that results in enlightenment.

73
00:04:46,268 --> 00:04:46,608
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not enough.

74
00:04:46,628 --> 00:04:49,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were, you would have been enlightened by now.

75
00:04:50,740 --> 00:05:14,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you haven't given up, given in to that, just that little, oh, but it's enough, this is the metta, it's good enough metta, as in it partakes to that which metta actually is, if you haven't given up just because of that discomfort, you would have actually, effortlessly, forced yourself in the direction of progress, in the direction of development, simply by not covering up the obvious truth.

76
00:05:14,911 --> 00:05:18,353
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't practice the dhamma unless I know what the dhamma is for myself, free from doubt.

77
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:20,813
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

78
00:05:21,834 --> 00:05:26,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's good to err on the side of, well, maybe this is not the exact matter.

79
00:05:26,377 --> 00:05:26,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

80
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:26,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

81
00:05:26,937 --> 00:05:27,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing to lose.

82
00:05:27,898 --> 00:05:28,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a win-win.

83
00:05:28,358 --> 00:05:28,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, we just push harder.

84
00:05:29,018 --> 00:05:31,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, we look deeper into the matter.

85
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:31,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

86
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:41,186
[SPEAKER_00]: And you revise that by, okay, this is matter for me at the moment, but is that the matter that the Buddha said matter is?

87
00:05:41,486 --> 00:05:43,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you read what the Buddha said matter is.

88
00:05:43,807 --> 00:05:44,528
[SPEAKER_00]: What results in?

89
00:05:45,525 --> 00:05:50,409
[SPEAKER_00]: What results in, to what extent it goes, and then you compare to where you are at.

90
00:05:50,589 --> 00:05:53,511
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I liable to future real world?

91
00:05:53,731 --> 00:05:53,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

92
00:05:54,952 --> 00:05:56,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Means this, what I have.

93
00:05:56,173 --> 00:06:00,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nobody denies what you have, feels good, it's probably useful to some extent.

94
00:06:00,916 --> 00:06:08,462
[SPEAKER_00]: But just don't think that it is what the Buddha was talking about, because if it were, you would be enlightened, you would be liberated, as that line said.

95
00:06:10,153 --> 00:06:15,055
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you say it is what the Buddha said, but I'm not liberated, you're misrepresenting the Buddha.

96
00:06:15,535 --> 00:06:23,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Erring on the side of caution doesn't mean perpetually keeping yourself in a state of doubt because I'm not certain about anything.

97
00:06:23,458 --> 00:06:29,860
[SPEAKER_00]: It means recognizing what certainty you have and then questioning that certainty further until you're more certain about it.

98
00:06:31,548 --> 00:06:34,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people are like, okay, so I mustn't jump into conclusions.

99
00:06:35,210 --> 00:06:37,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I will not discriminate anything I come across.

100
00:06:37,912 --> 00:06:38,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this might be fine.

101
00:06:39,153 --> 00:06:40,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this might be fine.

102
00:06:40,173 --> 00:06:41,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that might be fine.

103
00:06:41,494 --> 00:06:42,655
[SPEAKER_00]: This works for me, that will... No.

104
00:06:43,075 --> 00:06:46,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Universal truth, the dhamma, is the universal truth.

105
00:06:46,477 --> 00:06:49,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it's true in every scenario for every individual.

106
00:06:49,779 --> 00:06:52,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from suffering is always freedom from suffering.

107
00:06:52,361 --> 00:06:55,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about not committing to any direction.

108
00:06:55,915 --> 00:06:57,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's already the direction itself you committed.

109
00:06:58,136 --> 00:06:59,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I will not commit to any direction.

110
00:07:00,377 --> 00:07:07,939
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's more like recognizing, as I said, what you already know, what you're already doing, and just not taking it for granted, and questioning it further.

111
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:08,640
[SPEAKER_00]: That same thing.

112
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:13,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Not dismissing it, or, or, oh, I will not commit to it, because maybe it's wrong.

113
00:07:14,622 --> 00:07:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, everything is wrong for a Bhutujana.

114
00:07:16,703 --> 00:07:17,763
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to commit to something.

115
00:07:19,158 --> 00:07:21,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is not committing to a wrong thing.

116
00:07:21,099 --> 00:07:27,301
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is committing to a wrong thing and then not questioning the wrongness of it or abandoning the commitment to it.

117
00:07:27,321 --> 00:07:35,164
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I understood metta practice as one thing, but how is this and how will it free me from being liable to ill-will?

118
00:07:35,905 --> 00:07:36,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

119
00:07:36,685 --> 00:07:44,668
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that... And instead of now reading what metta is for you into the suttas, you want to do other way around.

120
00:07:44,788 --> 00:07:50,411
[SPEAKER_01]: And you shouldn't be afraid of questioning your practice, because that which is right cannot be undermined.

121
00:07:50,431 --> 00:07:51,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Being afraid is not a matter of choice.

122
00:07:52,572 --> 00:07:53,952
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be afraid.

123
00:07:53,972 --> 00:07:55,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you shouldn't be afraid.

124
00:07:55,333 --> 00:07:59,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you shouldn't, but you will be afraid, because you're undermining the very basis.

125
00:08:00,836 --> 00:08:02,377
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I meant.

126
00:08:02,417 --> 00:08:03,057
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people...

127
00:08:04,578 --> 00:08:05,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do it.

128
00:08:05,138 --> 00:08:15,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's good enough, because just that slight undermining reveals that underlying anxiety that you might be wrong, and all of your efforts might have been completely misguided.

129
00:08:16,886 --> 00:08:30,035
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you actually don't give in to that fear, that will have to arise, on account of questioning the very core of your whole commitment and everything, you will realize that, actually, no.

130
00:08:30,535 --> 00:08:34,158
[SPEAKER_00]: This is why I'm liable to fear and everything else, because I haven't been questioning this.

131
00:08:35,177 --> 00:08:44,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you practice 30 years of your practice wrongly, the true reason why that practice was wrong, is because you never questioned that core of it.

132
00:08:45,662 --> 00:08:52,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But the day you start questioning the core of it, even if it's after 30 years of wrong practice, everything will be made right.

133
00:08:52,526 --> 00:09:00,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I was trying to say, testing your... testing yourself, testing your understanding.

134
00:09:03,007 --> 00:09:08,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember that sutra that says that I treat... the Buddha says to the... yeah, I treat monks like a clay, like a potter treats his pots.

135
00:09:08,653 --> 00:09:08,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

136
00:09:09,674 --> 00:09:11,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you just have to define the testing though.

137
00:09:11,856 --> 00:09:18,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Testing does not mean exposing yourself to the circumstances that will compromise your precepts or your seclusion.

138
00:09:18,762 --> 00:09:19,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I need to test myself.

139
00:09:20,484 --> 00:09:22,566
[SPEAKER_00]: No, testing means reviewing your mind.

140
00:09:23,885 --> 00:09:28,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, oh, I've, like, I've established my mind in metta and I'm practicing metta.

141
00:09:29,166 --> 00:09:31,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, now question whether that that is metta.

142
00:09:31,487 --> 00:09:38,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just take it for granted and then do this metta you're practicing, but instead question whether that is metta that you're practicing.

143
00:09:42,049 --> 00:09:42,630
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's it.

144
00:09:42,670 --> 00:09:42,970
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that.

145
00:09:43,010 --> 00:09:43,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that first point.

146
00:09:45,073 --> 00:09:52,418
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the sutta, you hear the teaching, you get the idea, and then you start doing things on account of the idea, without questioning the idea.

147
00:09:52,858 --> 00:09:59,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have the other group of people, who now realize they need to question the idea, so they stop doing the idea, because they're not sure about the idea.

148
00:10:01,027 --> 00:10:01,147
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

149
00:10:01,547 --> 00:10:03,109
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to do the idea you got.

150
00:10:03,149 --> 00:10:06,552
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize that is the level you... that is the extent you got from it.

151
00:10:07,092 --> 00:10:16,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to do it, but at the same time, you want to keep the context that that idea might change, might be wrong, and might need further development.

152
00:10:17,401 --> 00:10:19,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will remain in the middle.

153
00:10:19,863 --> 00:10:28,851
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be blindsided by your own refusal to question it because it's uncomfortable and reveals the anxiety on account of that which you invested effort in already.

154
00:10:30,682 --> 00:10:34,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor will you go to that extreme and then not commit to anything, because you're not sure about anything.

155
00:10:36,064 --> 00:10:37,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're already committing to that.

156
00:10:37,884 --> 00:10:38,784
[SPEAKER_01]: You start somewhere.

157
00:10:39,265 --> 00:10:40,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I must only do good things.

158
00:10:40,545 --> 00:10:40,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

159
00:10:40,845 --> 00:10:41,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

160
00:10:41,265 --> 00:10:41,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

161
00:10:41,565 --> 00:10:41,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

162
00:10:42,286 --> 00:10:49,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start doing good things, you start keeping the precept, and then the idea behind that, you start questioning it, while still keeping the precept.

163
00:10:49,848 --> 00:10:50,968
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not, oh, I need to change.

164
00:10:50,988 --> 00:10:51,268
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

165
00:10:51,428 --> 00:10:53,529
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not questionable.

166
00:10:53,849 --> 00:10:56,030
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not up for debate.

167
00:10:56,330 --> 00:10:57,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Precepts must be kept.

168
00:10:57,310 --> 00:10:58,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensory strain must be developed.

169
00:10:58,991 --> 00:10:59,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't understand why.

170
00:11:00,838 --> 00:11:04,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so that's the idea I need to work on while I maintain these practices.

171
00:11:04,621 --> 00:11:07,984
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the testing of yourself is.

172
00:11:10,025 --> 00:11:15,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you shouldn't be afraid of that testing, because it can only... upgrading is not... Yeah, you will be afraid, but...

173
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:16,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, sorry.

174
00:11:16,391 --> 00:11:16,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

175
00:11:17,311 --> 00:11:17,912
[SPEAKER_01]: But you shouldn't be.

176
00:11:17,952 --> 00:11:20,134
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's exactly why people don't do it, because of fear.

177
00:11:20,234 --> 00:11:20,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

178
00:11:20,754 --> 00:11:22,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not... that's what I mean, it's not a method of choice.

179
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:25,598
[SPEAKER_00]: The anxiety is unpleasant, and that's why you don't question it.

180
00:11:26,899 --> 00:11:29,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So you will be afraid, and that's what the practice is.

181
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:47,948
[SPEAKER_00]: To accept that anxiety, that concern, that unease about, basically, like, you know, shaking up, moving around, seeing what's going to fall out and break, on account of your own very core of your views and values, without doing the opposite of those cores and values.

182
00:11:48,168 --> 00:11:54,651
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the precepts, the behavior that has been following the precepts and sense restraint, needs to be developed beforehand.

183
00:11:55,651 --> 00:11:59,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Because once you start, sort of, sieving through these values and revisiting them,

184
00:12:00,617 --> 00:12:02,638
[SPEAKER_00]: That behavior is already as a habit established.

185
00:12:02,798 --> 00:12:06,601
[SPEAKER_00]: The behavior of non... like five precepts, eight precepts, things you don't do.

186
00:12:07,381 --> 00:12:07,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

187
00:12:09,462 --> 00:12:15,026
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start questioning the values of the precepts, before you started keeping the precepts, oh, you will be left and right.

188
00:12:15,366 --> 00:12:17,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not going to be keeping anything.

189
00:12:17,527 --> 00:12:21,990
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be say no to everything and say yes to everything, just as the mind flips.

190
00:12:23,211 --> 00:12:28,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you need to first sustain yourself in the lifestyle of precepts, sense restraint,

191
00:12:30,643 --> 00:12:33,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, these layers of development will become more apparent.

192
00:12:35,066 --> 00:12:39,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can start questioning the values of it, without needing to go and do the opposite of that precept.

193
00:12:39,570 --> 00:12:41,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the precept is not up for question.

194
00:12:41,972 --> 00:12:42,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your value of it.

195
00:12:45,014 --> 00:12:47,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keeping the precepts is the behavior that must be maintained.

196
00:12:49,517 --> 00:12:49,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

197
00:12:51,059 --> 00:12:52,760
[SPEAKER_00]: That's now for you to discern.

198
00:12:54,934 --> 00:12:57,316
[SPEAKER_00]: But once people start questioning, why do I need a door?

199
00:12:57,336 --> 00:12:59,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't need to and then go and break the precepts.

200
00:13:00,198 --> 00:13:02,419
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not a good start.

201
00:13:07,663 --> 00:13:14,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, same, like whether it's metta practice, free from ill-will, all of that requires you to...

202
00:13:14,388 --> 00:13:15,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so I'm not engaging with ill-will.

203
00:13:18,576 --> 00:13:23,018
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that were the practice of metta, I would be free from future ill-will.

204
00:13:23,298 --> 00:13:24,998
[SPEAKER_00]: I would not be liable to ill-will.

205
00:13:25,118 --> 00:13:29,280
[SPEAKER_00]: I would be outside of the domain of the possibility of ill-will manifesting in my experience.

206
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:41,404
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not there, which means my practice is the level I am at, but that's not the metta that should be developed, because the metta that the Buddha said should be developed is the one that frees you from ill-will, through and through.

207
00:13:44,063 --> 00:13:44,943
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all you need to do.

208
00:13:45,143 --> 00:13:46,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep practicing at that level.

209
00:13:46,484 --> 00:13:55,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't conflate the two, because conflating will be done on account of that fear and anxiety of questioning that your metta might not be the metta that the Buddha talks about.

210
00:13:56,248 --> 00:14:01,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Or when people say you can be an arahant and still engage with sensuality.

211
00:14:01,690 --> 00:14:02,450
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing.

212
00:14:03,771 --> 00:14:04,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

213
00:14:04,891 --> 00:14:11,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, those people will not do it because they're malicious and intentionally want to misrepresent the Buddha.

214
00:14:11,974 --> 00:14:12,134
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

215
00:14:12,154 --> 00:14:12,975
[SPEAKER_00]: They just genuinely

216
00:14:14,017 --> 00:14:24,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Believe that the level they're at, because it feels right for them, and that's their criteria, not the criteria the Buddha said should be, such as freedom from suffering, freedom from liability to lust, ill-will, distraction.

217
00:14:25,761 --> 00:14:26,641
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it just feels right.

218
00:14:29,162 --> 00:14:34,643
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm practicing rightly, and at the same time, I am engaging in sensuality, so my experience is the criteria of what's right.

219
00:14:34,724 --> 00:14:39,385
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they make a statement such as, you can practice for arahantship and not be free from sensuality.

220
00:14:42,356 --> 00:14:44,642
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of doing the Buddhist practice, it's like, well, wait.

221
00:14:45,043 --> 00:14:46,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it your criteria or the Buddhist criteria?

222
00:14:47,088 --> 00:14:49,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you read the Buddhist criteria and he said the opposite of what you're claiming.

223
00:14:50,852 --> 00:14:54,673
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't engage with sensuality and practice for arahantship at the same time.

224
00:14:54,794 --> 00:14:55,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Mutually exclusive.

225
00:14:56,154 --> 00:15:01,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can either ignore that, or not read the suttas and claim you're practicing the Buddha's teaching, and then just follow your own criteria.

226
00:15:01,816 --> 00:15:03,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Criteria of the ignorant mind.

227
00:15:03,457 --> 00:15:05,918
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind that shies away from that anxiety of questioning.

228
00:15:06,598 --> 00:15:12,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and, like, by... the mind that basically, when undermines the value, undermines the behavior of the value.

229
00:15:14,181 --> 00:15:16,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, just flip-flops between the precepts and non-precepts.

